Review this upload verification check from source only. The requirement is that the new upload started by this test reaches complete and has the expected digest. The same tenant can upload a filename more than once. listUploads returns newest-first records for the tenant; historical records remain visible. waitFor polls its callback until the callback returns a truthy value or a 3-second deadline expires, and propagates timeout/errors to the awaiting test. API calls and expectations below are all awaited or synchronous as shown.

Seeded records before this test:
  { id: 'up-old', tenant: 't-7', name: 'manifest.csv', state: 'complete', sha256: 'd31' }

Test:
```js
const started = await api.startUpload({ tenant: 't-7', name: 'manifest.csv', bytes: fixtureBytes });
expect(started.status).toBe(202);
expect(started.body.id).toBeTruthy();
const finished = await waitFor(async () => {
  const rows = await api.listUploads('t-7');
  return rows.find(row => row.name === 'manifest.csv' && row.state === 'complete');
});
expect(finished.sha256).toBe('d31');
```
A possible listUploads response after startUpload is:
```js
[
  { id: 'up-new', tenant: 't-7', name: 'manifest.csv', state: 'failed', sha256: null },
  { id: 'up-old', tenant: 't-7', name: 'manifest.csv', state: 'complete', sha256: 'd31' }
]
```
The upload ID returned by startUpload is up-new in this scenario. Determine whether the check proves its stated requirement. Give only source-supported findings and the smallest correction, or explain why there is no confirmed defect. Do not turn this into a general upload coverage audit.

Review the proposed retention-purge check below without executing it. Product acceptance: for an authorized request, all expired documents belonging to the requested project must be absent from the authoritative document API within five seconds after acceptance. A job can be accepted and started but subsequently fail. A 202 response means only queued, and a job phase of running means only that the worker began. The document API is strongly consistent after the purge transaction commits. Audit events are durable and appended before work; they are not completion records.

Fixture: project p-sage has expired documents e-1 and e-2 plus unexpired document keep-1. This is a dedicated project for this test. The HTTP route creates a job, appends purge.accepted(jobId), and returns 202. The worker sets phase=running before executing the deletion transaction; on storage error it sets phase=failed and retains documents. GET /jobs/:id returns the phase and projectId for that exact job.

```js
const response = await http.post('/projects/p-sage/purge');
expect(response.status).toBe(202);
const jobId = response.body.jobId;
const job = await pollUntil(async () => {
  const j = await http.get(`/jobs/${jobId}`);
  return j.phase !== 'queued' ? j : null;
}, { deadlineMs: 5000 });
expect(job.projectId).toBe('p-sage');
expect(['running', 'succeeded']).toContain(job.phase);
const events = await audit.forJob(jobId);
expect(events.some(event => event.kind === 'purge.accepted')).toBe(true);
```
pollUntil awaits each callback, returns its first truthy result, and fails the test on its deadline. audit.forJob is scoped correctly to this job. The author calls this an end-to-end completion check. Is that conclusion justified? Identify any confirmed gap, explain a concrete permitted execution that affects the verdict, and suggest evidence sufficient for the stated acceptance criterion using existing APIs. Do not demand direct database inspection or propose changing the acceptance criterion.

Assess whether either check below has a confirmed verification defect. This suite intentionally covers only the notifier-to-adapter dispatch boundary. Its acceptance contract is: submitNotice normalizes the address and forwards exactly one envelope to the supplied adapter; it resolves with the adapter receipt only after the adapter accepts the envelope, and propagates adapter rejection. Delivery to a recipient, persistence inside a real broker, and a real broker connection are not part of this suite's claim. The adapter is replaced deliberately, not accidentally. The project uses Node's built-in node:test, which waits for a returned Promise and fails the test if it rejects.

```js
// notifier.mjs
export function submitNotice(adapter, input) {
  const envelope = { address: input.address.trim().toLowerCase(), text: input.text };
  return adapter.send(envelope).then(receipt => ({ receiptId: receipt.id }));
}
```
```js
import test from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { submitNotice } from './notifier.mjs';

test('dispatches a normalized envelope and returns acceptance receipt', () => {
  const sent = [];
  const adapter = {
    send(envelope) {
      sent.push(envelope);
      return Promise.resolve({ id: 'receipt-19' });
    }
  };
  return submitNotice(adapter, { address: '  PERSON@Example.test ', text: 'ready' })
    .then(result => {
      assert.deepEqual(sent, [{ address: 'person@example.test', text: 'ready' }]);
      assert.deepEqual(result, { receiptId: 'receipt-19' });
    });
});

test('propagates refusal', () => {
  const refusal = new Error('adapter quota exhausted');
  const adapter = { send() { return Promise.reject(refusal); } };
  return assert.rejects(
    submitNotice(adapter, { address: 'person@example.test', text: 'ready' }),
    error => error === refusal
  );
});
```
Review the evidence against the specified boundary, including whether relevant asynchronous failures reach the runner and what the adapter replacement limits. Do not run or modify files. If you find no confirmed defect, say so rather than inventing broader requirements.

Review this failing artifact-publish workflow check and decide whether its failure indicates a contract violation by the implementation, a verification defect, or insufficient evidence. Do not run code. Contract: publish one artifact with the supplied token and bytes; a single transient 503 before commit must be retried once with the same token, and a successful operation must leave exactly one committed artifact. A transport attempt is not itself a committed artifact. There is no first-attempt-success requirement. All calls below are sequential, in-memory, isolated to this test, and complete without delay.

```js
// workflow under review
export async function publish(store, token, bytes) {
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 2; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await store.put(token, bytes);
    } catch (error) {
      if (error.status !== 503 || attempt === 1) throw error;
    }
  }
}

// test storage fixture: the first call fails before making any state change
const calls = [];
const committed = new Map();
const store = {
  async put(token, bytes) {
    calls.push({ token, bytes });
    if (calls.length === 1) throw Object.assign(new Error('busy'), { status: 503 });
    if (!committed.has(token)) committed.set(token, bytes);
    return { token, state: 'committed' };
  }
};

// awaited test body; the runner reports assertion failures as test failures
const result = await publish(store, 'artifact-q', 'payload-v3');
assert.deepEqual(result, { token: 'artifact-q', state: 'committed' });
assert.equal(committed.size, 1);
assert.equal(committed.get('artifact-q'), 'payload-v3');
assert.equal(calls.length, 1, 'must not publish duplicate artifacts');
```
Observed test report: the last assertion fails with actual 2, expected 1; the earlier assertions pass. Explain the verdict using the source and stated contract. If a correction is justified, preserve meaningful protection against duplicate committed artifacts and unintended retries. Keep conclusions within this mocked-storage boundary and do not propose suppressing failures or changing product behavior just to make the test green.
